2026-05-25T02:35:09.204202Z  INFO ThreadId(17) universal_search_service::service: 683: Claude API response: {"content":[{"thinking":"","type":"thinking"},{"text":"Based on my research, here's a comprehensive overview of Google SRE runbook documentation structure best practices and infrastructure documentation organization:\n\n## Google SRE Runbook Documentation Structure Best Practices\n\n### Core Runbook Components\n\nAccording to Google's SRE Workbook and industry best practices, effective runbooks should include:\n\n**Essential Metadata:**\n- **Task ID** - Reference to incident/ticket system (e.g., INC-101)\n- **Task Name** - Brief description (2-3 words)\n- **Task Description** - High-level purpose without technical details\n- **Task Owner** - Responsible team member with contact info\n- **Team** - Which team executes the task\n- **Time Estimates** - Expected duration and actual timestamps\n- **Status** - Current state (ASSIGNED, IN_PROGRESS, BLOCKED, COMPLETE)\n\n**Procedural Content:**\n- **Objective** - Clear goal definition\n- **Step-by-Step Instructions** - Detailed, actionable steps with commands/scripts\n- **Verification Steps** - Checks to confirm resolution\n- **Troubleshooting Tips** - Common errors and solutions\n- **Contact Points** - Escalation contacts\n\n### Organizational Best Practices\n\n**Documentation Structure:**\n1. **Version Control** - Store runbooks in Git repositories for tracking changes\n2. **Centralized Repository** - Use platforms like GitHub, Confluence, or Notion\n3. **Consistent Formatting** - Standardized templates across all runbooks\n4. **Clear Naming Conventions** - Descriptive, searchable titles\n\n**Integration with SRE Workflow:**\n- **Monitoring Integration** - Link runbooks to alerting systems (PagerDuty, Prometheus)\n- **Automation Tools** - Connect with Rundeck, Ansible, AWS Systems Manager\n- **CI/CD Pipelines** - Integrate with deployment and rollback processes\n- **Postmortem Process** - Update runbooks based on incident learnings\n\n### Google's SRE Principles Applied to Runbooks\n\n**From the Google SRE Workbook:**\n- **Eliminate Toil** - Automate repetitive runbook steps where possible\n- **Embrace Risk** - Use runbooks to manage and mitigate operational risks\n- **Measure Reliability** - Include SLO/SLI monitoring in runbook procedures\n- **Blameless Culture** - Document procedures objectively without assigning fault\n\n**Key SRE Concepts in Documentation:**\n- **Service Level Objectives (SLOs)** - Define reliability targets in runbooks\n- **Error Budgets** - Reference error budget status in decision-making steps\n- **Incident Command System** - Include clear roles and communication channels\n- **Postmortem Templates** - Structured approach to learning from incidents\n\n### Automation Strategy\n\n**Progressive Automation Approach:**\n1. **Manual Execution First** - Perform tasks manually to understand the process\n2. **Document Clearly** - Add screenshots and diagrams for complex procedures\n3. **Validate Each Step** - Include verification points throughout\n4. **Automate Gradually** - Script repetitive steps using appropriate tools\n5. **Test Thoroughly** - Validate automation in staging before production use\n\n**Automation Tools:**\n- **Rundeck** - Job scheduling and execution\n- **Ansible** - Configuration management and orchestration\n- **AWS Systems Manager** - Cloud infrastructure automation\n- **Custom Scripts** - Python, Bash, or other languages for specific tasks\n\n### Infrastructure Documentation Organization\n\n**Hierarchical Structure:**\n```\n/runbooks\n  /incident-response\n    /network-outages\n    /database-failures\n    /security-incidents\n  /routine-maintenance\n    /server-updates\n    /certificate-renewals\n  /disaster-recovery\n    /region-failover\n    /data-restoration\n```\n\n**Cross-Referencing:**\n- Link related runbooks together\n- Reference system architecture diagrams\n- Connect to monitoring dashboards\n- Include dependency information\n\n### Maintenance and Governance\n\n**Regular Reviews:**\n- **Quarterly Audits** - Validate runbook accuracy and completeness\n- **Post-Incident Updates** - Incorporate lessons learned immediately\n- **Ownership Assignment** - Designate runbook maintainers\n- **Access Control** - Use RBAC to manage editing permissions\n\n**Quality Standards:**\n- **Conciseness** - Avoid unnecessary detail that obscures critical steps\n- **Clarity** - Use simple language and visual aids\n- **Testability** - Include validation steps for each major action\n- **Searchability** - Use consistent tags and metadata\n\n### Integration with Broader SRE Practices\n\n**Connection to Other SRE Artifacts:**\n- **Service Documentation** - Link to architecture and design docs\n- **Monitoring Configuration** - Reference alert definitions and dashboards\n- **Capacity Planning** - Include scaling procedures and thresholds\n- **Change Management** - Connect to deployment and rollback processes\n\nThis structured approach ensures runbooks are not just documentation, but living tools that enhance reliability, reduce MTTR, and support the overall SRE mission of building and maintaining robust, scalable systems.\n\n**Sources:**\n- Google SRE Workbook (https://sre.google/workbook/)\n- SolarWinds SRE Best Practices Guide\n- SRE School Comprehensive Runbook Tutorial","type":"text"}],"id":"1221070b1406469bb1abb03d4e8108fe","model":"kat-coder-pro-v2","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"stop_sequence","stop_sequence":"248046","type":"message","usage":{"cache_read_input_tokens":127384,"input_tokens":4033,"output_tokens":1097}}
