2026-06-03T02:24:24.577086Z  INFO ThreadId(12) universal_search_service::service: 685: Claude API response: {"content":[{"thinking":"","type":"thinking"},{"text":"Based on my research, I can provide you with information about these two important works in equivariant deep learning:\n\n## SE(3)-Transformer\n\n**SE(3)-Transformer** is a neural network architecture introduced by **Fabian B. Fuchs, Daniel E. Worrall, Volker Fischer, and Max Welling** in their 2020 NeurIPS paper \"SE(3)-Transformers: 3D Roto-Translation Equivariant Attention Networks\" [arXiv:2006.10503](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.10503).\n\nKey features:\n- A self-attention mechanism for 3D point clouds and graphs that is equivariant under continuous 3D roto-translations (SE(3) group)\n- Leverages self-attention benefits while guaranteeing SE(3)-equivariance for robustness\n- Uses spherical harmonics and tensor field network concepts\n- Outperforms non-equivariant attention baselines and equivariant models without attention\n- Evaluated on N-body simulations, ScanObjectNN (3D object classification), and QM9 (molecular property prediction)\n\n## Victor Garcia Satorras and EGNN\n\n**Victor Garcia Satorras** is the lead author of **E(n)-Equivariant Graph Neural Networks (EGNN)**, introduced in the 2021 ICML paper \"E(n) Equivariant Graph Neural Networks\" with Emiel Hoogeboom and Max Welling [arXiv:2102.09844](https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.09844).\n\nKey features of EGNN:\n- Graph neural networks equivariant to rotations, translations, reflections, and permutations\n- Unlike SE(3)-Transformer, EGNN does **not** require computationally expensive higher-order representations in intermediate layers\n- Easily scaled to higher-dimensional spaces (not limited to 3D)\n- Achieves competitive or better performance than existing methods while being more efficient\n- Demonstrated on dynamical systems modeling, graph autoencoders, and molecular property prediction\n\n## Relationship Between the Two\n\nBoth works address equivariant deep learning for geometric data, but with different approaches:\n- **SE(3)-Transformer** uses attention mechanisms with tensor field network components\n- **EGNN** provides a simpler, more efficient approach that operates on scalar features and distances\n\nThe EGNN paper directly compares against SE(3)-Transformer as a baseline in their N-body experiments, showing EGNN's superior efficiency while maintaining competitive performance.\n\nBoth architectures have been highly influential in the development of geometric deep learning and equivariant neural networks for scientific applications.","type":"text"}],"id":"c2b5c7779d4641c28c0a30d559f528b6","model":"kat-coder-pro-v2","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"stop_sequence","stop_sequence":"248046","type":"message","usage":{"cache_read_input_tokens":64320,"input_tokens":14815,"output_tokens":569}}
