2026-06-05T14:00:39.309038Z  INFO ThreadId(11) universal_search_service::service: 685: Claude API response: {"content":[{"thinking":"","type":"thinking"},{"text":"Based on my research, here's a comprehensive guide on converting multimodal projector (vision tower) models from safetensors to GGUF format using llama.cpp:\n\n## Converting Multimodal Projector to GGUF\n\n### Method 1: Using llama.cpp's Official Conversion Script\n\nThe primary method is to use the `convert_hf_to_gguf.py` script with the `--mmproj` flag. You need to run the conversion **twice**:\n\n**Step 1: Convert the main text model**\n```bash\npython convert_hf_to_gguf.py /path/to/model_directory \\\n  --outfile model.gguf \\\n  --outtype f16\n```\n\n**Step 2: Convert the multimodal projector**\n```bash\npython convert_hf_to_gguf.py /path/to/model_directory \\\n  --mmproj \\\n  --outfile mmproj-model.gguf \\\n  --outtype f16\n```\n\nThe `--mmproj` flag tells the converter to extract only the vision encoder and projection layers, creating a separate GGUF file for the multimodal components.\n\n### Example: Qwen2.5-VL-7B\n\nFrom the official llama.cpp discussion, here's the exact command sequence:\n\n```bash\n# Convert main model\npython convert_hf_to_gguf.py Qwen/Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct \\\n  --outfile Qwen2.5-VL-7B.gguf \\\n  --outtype f16\n\n# Convert multimodal projector\npython convert_hf_to_gguf.py Qwen/Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct \\\n  --mmproj \\\n  --outfile mmproj-Qwen2.5-VL-7B.gguf \\\n  --outtype f16\n```\n\n### Supported Models\n\nThe `--mmproj` conversion works for vision-language models that have a `vision_encoder` component in their configuration. Supported architectures include:\n\n- LLaVA 1.5/1.6\n- Gemma 3\n- MiniCPM-V\n- GraniteVision\n- MobileVLM\n- Qwen2-VL / Qwen2.5-VL\n- Pixtral\n- InternVL\n- Phi-3/4 multimodal\n- And many others\n\n### Important Notes\n\n1. **Output file naming**: When using `--mmproj`, an \"mmproj-\" prefix is automatically added to the output filename if you don't specify one.\n\n2. **Architecture detection**: The converter automatically detects the model architecture and extracts the appropriate vision components.\n\n3. **Quantization**: The mmproj file can be quantized separately using `llama-quantize`, but note that some vision encoders (like SigLIP) may have dimension constraints that limit quantization options.\n\n4. **Usage**: After conversion, use both files together:\n   ```bash\n   llama-server -m model.gguf --mmproj mmproj-model.gguf\n   ```\n\n### Alternative Tools\n\nIf you encounter issues with the official script, there are third-party tools like:\n- `safetensors_to_gguf` toolkit: https://github.com/elementalcollision/safetensorstogguf\n- Custom converters for specific model families\n\n### Limitations\n\n- Audio components are typically **not** converted to GGUF (no `audioproj` equivalent yet)\n- Some models may require custom handling\n- The conversion only works for models where the vision encoder is properly defined in the configuration\n\nThe process is straightforward for most modern vision-language models, and the official llama.cpp converter handles the complexity of extracting the correct components automatically.","type":"text"}],"id":"7bcedbfb80d44512a2bcbf277f9e2669","model":"kat-coder-pro-v2","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"stop_sequence","stop_sequence":"248046","type":"message","usage":{"cache_read_input_tokens":36804,"input_tokens":9177,"output_tokens":774}}
